#!/bin/sh
# moneroid-msr apply|restore|preset — the RandomX "MSR mod" that XMRig applies itself only
# when it runs as root; Moneroid runs it unprivileged, so moneroid-msr.service does it instead.
# Registers, values and the CPU mapping are XMRig 6.26.0's (src/crypto/rx/RxConfig.cpp,
# src/backend/cpu/platform/BasicCpuInfo.cpp). Needs msr-tools and the msr kernel module.
#   apply    save the current values (once per boot) and write the preset to every CPU
#   restore  write the saved values back to every CPU
#   preset   print the preset for this CPU ("register value [kept-bit]"), exit 1 if none
set -eu
PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
SAVED=/run/moneroid-msr.saved

cpu_field() { sed -n "s/^$1[[:space:]]*: //p" /proc/cpuinfo | head -n 1; }

# A third column is a bit kept from the current value: XMRig writes 0xc0011021 under the mask ~0x20.
preset() {
	case $(cpu_field vendor_id) in
	GenuineIntel) echo '0x1a4 0xf' ;;
	AuthenticAMD)
		# /proc/cpuinfo has family and model in decimal: 0x17 = 23, 0x19 = 25, 0x1a = 26, 0x61 = 97, 0x75 = 117.
		case $(cpu_field 'cpu family') in
		23) printf '%s\n' '0xc0011020 0x0' '0xc0011021 0x40 5' '0xc0011022 0x1510000' '0xc001102b 0x2000cc16' ;; # Zen, Zen+, Zen 2
		25) case $(cpu_field model) in
			97 | 117) printf '%s\n' '0xc0011020 0x4400000000000' '0xc0011021 0x4000000000040 5' '0xc0011022 0x8680000401570000' '0xc001102b 0x2040cc10' ;; # Zen 4
			*) printf '%s\n' '0xc0011020 0x4480000000000' '0xc0011021 0x1c000200000040 5' '0xc0011022 0xc000000401570000' '0xc001102b 0x2000cc10' ;; # Zen 3
			esac ;;
		26) printf '%s\n' '0xc0011020 0x4400000000000' '0xc0011021 0x4000000000040 5' '0xc0011022 0x8680000401570000' '0xc001102b 0x2040cc10' ;; # Zen 5
		*) return 1 ;;
		esac ;;
	*) return 1 ;;
	esac
}

cpus() { for d in /dev/cpu/[0-9]*; do echo "${d##*/}"; done; }

snapshot_ok() { # every preset register once, each with a hex value
	[ -s "$SAVED" ] || return 1
	[ "$(awk 'NF != 2 || $2 !~ /^0x[0-9a-f]+$/ { bad = 1 } END { print NR + 0, bad + 0 }' "$SAVED")" = "$(echo "$1" | wc -l) 0" ]
}

case ${1:-} in
preset) preset ;;
apply)
	p=$(preset) || { echo "moneroid-msr: no MSR preset for this CPU" >&2; exit 1; }
	# The first apply of a boot keeps the firmware values; a repeated apply must not save the modded ones.
	if [ ! -e "$SAVED" ]; then
		: > "$SAVED.tmp"
		for reg in $(echo "$p" | cut -d ' ' -f 1); do
			old=$(rdmsr -p 0 -c "$reg") || { rm -f "$SAVED.tmp"; echo "moneroid-msr: cannot read $reg" >&2; exit 1; }
			echo "$reg $old" >> "$SAVED.tmp"
		done
		mv "$SAVED.tmp" "$SAVED"
	fi
	snapshot_ok "$p" || { echo "moneroid-msr: $SAVED is incomplete or malformed; reboot to start clean" >&2; exit 1; }
	# A failure here fails the unit; ExecStopPost then writes the saved values back.
	echo "$p" | while read -r reg value keep; do
		if [ -z "$keep" ]; then wrmsr -a "$reg" "$value"; continue; fi
		# Only that bit is read (a full value may exceed the shell's signed 64-bit arithmetic);
		# the preset values have it clear and stay below 2^63.
		for c in $(cpus); do
			bit=$(rdmsr -p "$c" -u -f "$keep:$keep" "$reg")
			wrmsr -p "$c" "$reg" "$(printf '0x%x' $((value | (bit << keep))))"
		done
	done
	echo "moneroid-msr: preset applied ($(echo "$p" | wc -l) registers, originals in $SAVED)"
	;;
restore)
	[ -e "$SAVED" ] || { echo "moneroid-msr: nothing saved, nothing to restore"; exit 0; }
	# Every register on every CPU is tried; the snapshot goes only when all of them were written back.
	failed=0
	while read -r reg value; do
		case $value in 0x[0-9a-f]*) ;; *) failed=1; continue ;; esac
		for c in $(cpus); do wrmsr -p "$c" "$reg" "$value" || failed=1; done
	done < "$SAVED"
	[ $failed = 0 ] || { echo "moneroid-msr: some values were not restored; $SAVED kept" >&2; exit 1; }
	rm -f "$SAVED"
	echo "moneroid-msr: original values restored"
	;;
*) echo "usage: moneroid-msr apply|restore|preset" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
